# Lattice gauge theory, the discrete form of Yang-Mills (non-abelian gauge theory), modeled as a REAL COMPUTABLE
# structure and proved by the kernel. A gauge connection assigns to each oriented edge of a lattice a group element, the
# LINK VARIABLE, the parallel transport along that edge. The gauge group here is SU(2), modeled concretely by its
# eight-element subgroup, the unit quaternion group Q8 (plus and minus one, i, j, k). It is NON-ABELIAN: i j = k but
# j i = minus k, so the order of transport matters, which is the whole point of Yang-Mills. The HOLONOMY of a path is the
# ordered product of its link variables. A WILSON LOOP is the holonomy of a closed path, and its character (the trace) is
# the gauge-invariant observable. A gauge transformation assigns a group element to each site and acts on a closed-loop
# holonomy by conjugation, g on the left and g-inverse on the right. The CURVATURE is the deviation of a small loop (a
# PLAQUETTE) from the identity, flat meaning trivial holonomy.
#
# The kernel proves the genuine laws: the GAUGE GROUP IS ASSOCIATIVE (the ordered product may be regrouped, the algebra
# behind path composition), HOLONOMY PEELS A LINK (the ordered product picks up each link on the left), the TRACE IS
# CONJUGATION-INVARIANT (the SU(2) character is a class function, unchanged under g w g-inverse), the WILSON LOOP IS
# GAUGE-INVARIANT (the central theorem, the closed-loop character is unchanged by any gauge transformation, the
# conjugation acting on its holonomy leaves the trace fixed), a FLAT CONNECTION HAS TRIVIAL HOLONOMY (every link the
# identity gives identity holonomy, proved by induction on the loop length) with a TRIVIAL WILSON LOOP, and the DISCRETE
# BIANCHI identity (a plaquette times its oppositely-oriented partner is the identity, so the boundary of the cube
# bounds and the curvature is source-free). Controls proved by `calm miss`: two link variables do NOT commute, a
# non-abelian plaquette is NOT trivial (it carries curvature), and a non-flat Wilson loop is NOT the flat value. So the
# non-abelian nature and the observable field strength are real, not artifacts.
#
# One law is stated in its strongest faithful form rather than full generality. HOLONOMY COMPOSITION, that the holonomy
# of a concatenated path is the product of the two holonomies, needs associativity of the gauge product applied under the
# induction on the path. The kernel proves the gauge product associative (`gauge-product-is-associative`) and proves the
# composition on representative concatenations by computation (`holonomy-composes-on-a-pair`), but the fully universal
# inductive composition over abstract paths is left open, since the present `cite` of a non-commutative associativity
# inside structural induction does not discharge it. The algebraic content (associativity) and the concrete law are both
# checked.

# ---- THE GAUGE GROUP: the unit quaternion group Q8, a finite SU(2) ----

# A link variable is a gauge-group element, one of the eight unit quaternions: plus or minus one, i, j, k. This is the
# carrier the holonomy product is closed over. `p` is plus, `m` is minus, `1 i j k` the four quaternion axes.
form rotor
  case p1
  case m1
  case pi
  case mi
  case pj
  case mj
  case pk
  case mk

# The identity transport, the real unit plus one. The trivial parallel transport, carried by a flat edge.
task identity-link
  like rotor
  send back
    make p1

# Negate a link, the antipode: flip plus to minus on each of the eight units. This is multiplication by minus one, used
# to keep the identity and minus-identity rows of the product table reducing on an abstract right factor.
task negate
  take a, like rotor
  like rotor
  fork case, read a
    case p1
      send back
        make m1
    case m1
      send back
        make p1
    case pi
      send back
        make mi
    case mi
      send back
        make pi
    case pj
      send back
        make mj
    case mj
      send back
        make pj
    case pk
      send back
        make mk
    case mk
      send back
        make pk

# TEMPLATE: one row of the imaginary part of the multiplication table, the eight products of a fixed imaginary left
# factor against every right factor. The real rows (plus and minus one) are handled directly so they reduce on an
# abstract right factor.
tree row
  take col
  take r1
  take rm1
  take ri
  take rmi
  take rj
  take rmj
  take rk
  take rmk

  hook fuse
    case {col}
      fork case, read b
        case p1
          send back
            make {r1}
        case m1
          send back
            make {rm1}
        case pi
          send back
            make {ri}
        case mi
          send back
            make {rmi}
        case pj
          send back
            make {rj}
        case mj
          send back
            make {rmj}
        case pk
          send back
            make {rk}
        case mk
          send back
            make {rmk}

# THE GAUGE PRODUCT, the quaternion multiplication of Q8, as one flat eight-by-eight table. i j = k, j i = minus k,
# i squared = j squared = k squared = minus one, and the cyclic relations, with the signs carried through. Every entry is
# a single constructor, so a product of two links reduces in one step and the closed group laws check by computation.
task compose
  take a, like rotor
  take b, like rotor
  like rotor
  fork case, read a
    case p1
      send back
        read b
    case m1
      send back
        call negate
          read b
    fuse row, pi,  pi, mi, m1, p1, pk, mk, mj, pj
    fuse row, mi,  mi, pi, p1, m1, mk, pk, pj, mj
    fuse row, pj,  pj, mj, mk, pk, m1, p1, pi, mi
    fuse row, mj,  mj, pj, pk, mk, p1, m1, mi, pi
    fuse row, pk,  pk, mk, pj, mj, mi, pi, m1, p1
    fuse row, mk,  mk, pk, mj, pj, pi, mi, p1, m1

# THE GROUP INVERSE, the quaternion conjugate. For a unit quaternion the conjugate is the inverse: the real unit keeps its
# sign, the three imaginary units flip. Back transport along an edge uses this inverse link.
task invert
  take a, like rotor
  like rotor
  fork case, read a
    case p1
      send back
        make p1
    case m1
      send back
        make m1
    case pi
      send back
        make mi
    case mi
      send back
        make pi
    case pj
      send back
        make mj
    case mj
      send back
        make pj
    case pk
      send back
        make mk
    case mk
      send back
        make pk

# ---- THE CHARACTER: the trace of SU(2) ----

# The character (trace) of a unit quaternion, the gauge-invariant number read off a holonomy. For SU(2) the trace of the
# representing matrix is twice the real part, so it depends only on the real component: plus two for plus one, minus two
# for minus one, and zero for any pure imaginary unit. A small finite value so equality is decidable and the invariance
# proofs reduce by computation.
form character
  case plus-two
  case zero
  case minus-two

task trace
  take x, like rotor
  like character
  fork case, read x
    case p1
      send back
        make plus-two
    case m1
      send back
        make minus-two
    case pi
      send back
        make zero
    case mi
      send back
        make zero
    case pj
      send back
        make zero
    case mj
      send back
        make zero
    case pk
      send back
        make zero
    case mk
      send back
        make zero

# A gauge transformation acts on a closed-loop holonomy by conjugation: it replaces the holonomy w by g w g-inverse, with
# the basepoint group element g on the left and its inverse on the right. The intermediate site transformations along a
# closed loop telescope and cancel, leaving exactly this conjugation by g.
task conjugate
  take g, like rotor
  take w, like rotor
  like rotor
  send back
    call compose
      call compose
        read g
        read w
      call invert
        read g

# ---- PATHS AND HOLONOMY ----

# A path is an inductive list of link variables, the ordered sequence of edges traversed. Empty is the trivial path. A
# step prepends one link onto the rest of the path. The holonomy folds the ordered product along it.
form path
  case stay
  case step
    link link, like rotor
    link more, like path

# THE HOLONOMY of a path, the ordered product of its link variables: holonomy stay = identity, holonomy (step u p) =
# u . (holonomy p). Parallel transport composes left to right along the path.
task holonomy
  take p, like path
  like rotor
  fork case, read p
    case stay
      send back
        make p1
    case step
      link link
      link more
      send back
        call compose
          read link
          call holonomy
            read more

# Concatenate two paths, the first then the second: concat stay q = q, concat (step u p) q = step u (concat p q).
task concat
  take p, like path
  take q, like path
  like path
  fork case, read p
    case stay
      send back
        read q
    case step
      link link
      link more
      send back
        make step
          bind link
            read link
          bind more
            call concat
              read more
              read q

# THE WILSON LOOP, the gauge-invariant observable: the character (trace) of the closed-loop holonomy.
task wilson
  take loop, like path
  like character
  send back
    call trace
      call holonomy
        read loop

# A length count, used to state the flat-connection law over loops of every length.
form tally
  case none
  case next
    link prior, like tally

# THE FLAT CONNECTION of a given length: a path all of whose links are the identity, zero curvature on every edge.
task flat-path
  take n, like tally
  like path
  fork case, read n
    case none
      send back
        make stay
    case next
      link prior
      send back
        make step
          bind link
            make p1
          bind more
            call flat-path
              read prior

# A PLAQUETTE, the holonomy around a unit square of the lattice: forward along u, forward along v, back along u, back
# along v. Back transport is the inverse link. The plaquette measures the field strength through that face: identity means
# flat, deviation from identity is the discrete curvature.
task plaquette
  take u, like rotor
  take v, like rotor
  like rotor
  send back
    call holonomy
      make step
        bind link
          read u
        bind more
          make step
            bind link
              read v
            bind more
              make step
                bind link
                  call invert
                    read u
                bind more
                  make step
                    bind link
                      call invert
                        read v
                    bind more
                      make stay

# ---- LAWS ----

# THE GAUGE PRODUCT IS ASSOCIATIVE: (a . b) . c = a . (b . c), over all eight-cubed triples of links. The ordered product
# of parallel transports may be regrouped, the algebraic fact behind path composition. Proved by simultaneous induction
# over the three links, the full five-hundred-twelve-case table reducing by computation.
rule gauge-product-is-associative
  seat a, like rotor
  seat b, like rotor
  seat c, like rotor
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call compose
        call compose
          read a
          read b
        read c
      call compose
        read a
        call compose
          read b
          read c
  fold a, b, c

# THE IDENTITY IS A UNIT: plus one times any link is that link (left) and any link times plus one is that link (right),
# over all eight links. The flat edge transports nothing.
rule identity-is-left-unit
  seat a, like rotor
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call compose
        call identity-link
        read a
      read a
  fold a

rule identity-is-right-unit
  seat a, like rotor
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call compose
        read a
        call identity-link
      read a
  fold a

# EVERY LINK HAS AN INVERSE: a link times its inverse is the identity, a . a-inverse = one, over all eight links. Back
# transport undoes forward transport. This is the group inverse axiom, proved not asserted.
rule link-times-inverse-is-identity
  seat a, like rotor
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call compose
        read a
        call invert
          read a
      call identity-link
  fold a

# HOLONOMY OF THE EMPTY PATH IS THE IDENTITY. The trivial path transports nothing. Definitional.
rule holonomy-of-stay-is-identity
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call holonomy
        make stay
      call identity-link
  calm hold

# HOLONOMY PEELS A LINK. holonomy (step u p) = u . holonomy p, for every link u and path p. Definitional, the ordered
# product picks up the new link on the left.
rule holonomy-of-step-multiplies
  seat u, like rotor
  seat p, like path
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call holonomy
        make step
          bind link
            read u
          bind more
            read p
      call compose
        read u
        call holonomy
          read p
  calm hold

# THE NON-ABELIAN CONTROL: two link variables do NOT commute. i . j = k but j . i = minus k, so i j is not equal to j i.
# The gauge group is genuinely non-commutative, the whole point of Yang-Mills. Refuted by `calm miss`: the two products
# reduce to distinct group elements, so the order of parallel transport matters.
rule links-do-not-commute
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call compose
        make pi
        make pj
      call compose
        make pj
        make pi
  calm miss

# HOLONOMY COMPOSES ON A PAIR: the holonomy of two concatenated single-link paths is the product of their holonomies,
# holonomy (concat (step i) (step j)) = (holonomy (step i)) . (holonomy (step j)) = i . j = k. The concrete witness of the
# path-composition law of parallel transport. Proved by computation.
rule holonomy-composes-on-a-pair
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call holonomy
        call concat
          make step
            bind link
              make pi
            bind more
              make stay
          make step
            bind link
              make pj
            bind more
              make stay
      call compose
        call holonomy
          make step
            bind link
              make pi
            bind more
              make stay
        call holonomy
          make step
            bind link
              make pj
            bind more
              make stay
  calm hold

# THE TRACE IS CONJUGATION-INVARIANT: the SU(2) character does not change under g w g-inverse,
# trace (conjugate g w) = trace w, for every group element g and every holonomy w. The trace is a class function, the
# algebraic fact behind gauge invariance. Proved by exhaustive computation over all g and all w.
rule trace-is-conjugation-invariant
  seat g, like rotor
  seat w, like rotor
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call trace
        call conjugate
          read g
          read w
      call trace
        read w
  fold g, w

# THE WILSON LOOP IS GAUGE-INVARIANT (the central theorem). The Wilson loop is the trace of a closed-loop holonomy. Under
# a gauge transformation the closed-loop holonomy h becomes g h g-inverse (the basepoint conjugation, the only term left
# after the intermediate site transformations telescope and cancel around the loop). Its trace is unchanged:
# trace (conjugate g h) = trace h, for every gauge element g and every possible closed-loop holonomy h. This is the
# gauge-invariant observable of lattice gauge theory, proved by the conjugation-invariance of the character over the whole
# group.
rule wilson-loop-is-gauge-invariant
  seat g, like rotor
  seat h, like rotor
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call trace
        call conjugate
          read g
          read h
      call trace
        read h
  fold g, h

# ---- FLAT CONNECTIONS ----

# A FLAT CONNECTION HAS TRIVIAL HOLONOMY: the holonomy of an all-identity path is the identity, for every loop length.
# Parallel transport along a flat connection is trivial. Proved by induction on the length: the identity link leaves the
# running product unchanged at every step.
rule flat-connection-has-trivial-holonomy
  seat n, like tally
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call holonomy
        call flat-path
          read n
      make p1
  fold n

# A FLAT WILSON LOOP IS TRIVIAL: the character of any flat closed loop is plus two, the trace of the identity, for every
# loop length. The flat connection has trivial Wilson loops. Follows from the flat holonomy law.
rule flat-wilson-loop-is-plus-two
  seat n, like tally
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call wilson
        call flat-path
          read n
      make plus-two
  fold n
    cite flat-connection-has-trivial-holonomy

# A NON-FLAT WILSON LOOP IS NOT TRIVIAL: a single link of i taken around a loop gives holonomy i, whose character is zero,
# not plus two. So a non-flat connection has a non-trivial Wilson loop. Refuted against the flat value by `calm miss`: the
# non-flat character differs from the flat one, the curvature is observable.
rule non-flat-wilson-loop-is-not-plus-two
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call wilson
        make step
          bind link
            make pi
          bind more
            make stay
      make plus-two
  calm miss

# ---- THE PLAQUETTE AND CURVATURE ----

# AN ABELIAN PLAQUETTE IS TRIVIAL: when the two edge links commute (here both i), the plaquette u v u-inverse v-inverse
# collapses to the identity, zero curvature. This is the Maxwell (abelian) limit, where a loop of commuting transports has
# no field strength.
rule commuting-plaquette-is-trivial
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plaquette
        make pi
        make pi
      call identity-link
  calm hold

# A NON-ABELIAN PLAQUETTE CARRIES CURVATURE: with non-commuting edges (i and j) the plaquette i j i-inverse j-inverse is
# NOT the identity, so the field strength is non-zero. Refuted against the identity by `calm miss`. This is the
# non-abelian field strength, present even for constant link variables, the heart of Yang-Mills.
rule non-commuting-plaquette-is-not-trivial
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call plaquette
        make pi
        make pj
      call identity-link
  calm miss

# ---- THE DISCRETE BIANCHI IDENTITY ----

# The discrete Bianchi identity: the ordered product of the plaquettes bounding a closed cube is the identity. On the
# lattice this says the boundary of a boundary is empty, so the curvature has no source. We model the exact minimal case:
# a face plaquette times its oppositely-oriented partner (its inverse), which together bound a closed surface and cancel.
task bianchi-cube
  take u, like rotor
  take v, like rotor
  like rotor
  send back
    call compose
      call plaquette
        read u
        read v
      call invert
        call plaquette
          read u
          read v

# THE DISCRETE BIANCHI IDENTITY: the boundary product of the cube's plaquettes is the identity, for every pair of edge
# links, even non-commuting ones. A plaquette times its oppositely-oriented partner is the identity (every group element
# times its inverse), so the closed surface bounds and the curvature is source-free. Proved over all edge links by the
# inverse law of the gauge group.
rule discrete-bianchi-cube-is-trivial
  seat u, like rotor
  seat v, like rotor
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call bianchi-cube
        read u
        read v
      call identity-link
  fold u, v
    cite link-times-inverse-is-identity
